    "pad_tides": "Tides rise and fall twice a day along most coastlines. The pull of the moon draws the ocean into a gentle bulge on the side of the planet facing it, and a second bulge forms on the far side. As the planet turns, each stretch of shore passes through both bulges, so the water climbs the beach and then retreats. When the sun and moon line up, the tides are larger than usual; when they pull at right angles, the range is smaller. Harbors keep printed tables of these times so boats can enter and leave safely.",
    "pad_bread": "Bread dough rises because yeast feeds on the sugars in flour and releases small bubbles of gas. Kneading stretches the proteins in the flour into long, elastic strands that trap those bubbles, so the dough swells instead of letting the gas escape. A warm kitchen speeds the process, while a cool one slows it down and can deepen the flavor. In the oven, the trapped gas expands once more before the crust sets, which is why a loaf grows during the first minutes of baking and then holds its shape. Bakers often cut a shallow line across the top of the loaf so that it can expand evenly as it bakes.",
    "pad_lighthouse": "Lighthouses were built on rocky points and harbor entrances to warn ships at night. Early towers burned wood or coal in open fires, which were hard to see in rain or fog. Later designs used oil lamps placed behind curved mirrors, and then large glass lenses that bent the light into a narrow, powerful beam. Each tower was given its own pattern of flashes, so sailors could tell one light from another along the coast. Today many of these towers run automatically and are maintained by small crews. Some of the oldest towers have since become small museums that are open to visitors."
